## Reach

On one occasion, a woman I knew spent five minutes directing my attention, through an avalanche of words, intensely toward my right hand. At the end of it I felt every bone, each joint, I felt the blood running through my veins and skin wrapping around the flesh. The right hand felt very different from the left. Attention is powerful.

> In order to do anything that matters, we must first be able to give attention to the things that matter. Doing so has never been easy, but lately it’s become harder in new and surprising ways
~ [James Williams (2018)](https://www.cambridge.org/core/services/aop-cambridge-core/content/view/3F8D7BA2C0FE3A7126A4D9B73A89415D/9781108429092AR.pdf/Stand_out_of_our_Light.pdf)

In Latin, *attendere* means to stretch. To reach. But while stretching is a slow, deliberate movement, scrolling reduced my attention to a twitch. Just a quick reflex, a spasm designed to hijack my brain's reward mechanism. It forced me to trade intention for impulse. Hijacked attention getting hooked on the tiny dopamine shots I got everytime I glimpsed something I liked.

In the short term, giving attention to Instagram was distracting me from what I actually wanted to do: sew a dress. Practice the piano. Go for a run. But in the long term, it was chipping away at my ability to pursue and achieve goals that really matter to me: Make my own clothes. Play the Gnossienne. Be fit.

Because even an 30 minutes of my daily attention directed toward the feed was too much. And I did miss it. Attention allows me to focus and so learn, create, and connect. It amplifies my senses, so I can truly see, feel, and smell, and, just as importantly, be seen, felt (and smelled) by others.

Scrolling Instagram feed I instead gave attention to Meta and serving their goal, which is dead simple: selling me advertising.

