Instagram promises to "[brings you closer to the people and things you love](https://about.instagram.com/)". But does it really?

In my experience, the feed does not really feel nourishing. It’s even called a feed. Get it? Fodder. Much of what I was being served feelt more like fast food than a nourishing meal. Images of bikers, hikers, nature. Excercise. Cool buildings. Impressive art. Pretty objects, good-looking people, cute animals.

But what I truly want are experiences that fill me up and satisfy my needs: feet in the grass, a laugh with a friend, playing an instrument, walking in the forest, the smell of food being prepared.

The Instagram experience is instead packed with empty calories, that just as real fast food, left me craving for more. And that is part of the business model.

Targeting me with an ad for anti-ageing cream does not make much sense if I do not care about wrinkles. They can not sell me a solution for a problem I don't think I have, but they can manufacture the feeling that something is missing.

Make me compare my body to other bodies. My home to other homes. My life to other lives. Show me experiences I am not having, places I am not visiting, things I do not own, people I do not look like. It holds a carrot on a stick that will never be close enough to eat.

So this compulsive feeding of my brain with images from an algorithmic feed was not merely distracting. It was manipulative. Every day, I was reaching for a system designed to hijack my attention, pull me away from my own goals, and keep me hungry.

In the end, I was the one being consumed.

<SubscribeInline />

## Release

Commercial social media are currently a toxic environment, that we don't really chose, but are instead forced into by peer pressure. But it does not have to be this way. We can make spaces where the algorithm aims to make us calmer. More educated. More empathetic. More cooperative. Or where it does nothing at all.

For me, richness in life comes from movement. Being up and about with wind in my hair, dancing, fingers in soil, or shaping clay. Senses touching the environment, smoke from a fire, warmth on my skin, blackbird's song on the air.

Getting sucked into a feed I stop breathing, stop feeling thirst. I stop feeling like a body in a world. Moreover, I spend time in an environment where the engineers don't give a damn about my well being. Where I am being actively manipulated. That is made by a company that has [repeatedly been sued](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lawsuits_involving_Meta_Platforms) for fueling addiction and knowingly harming their users mental health.

So I left. My thumb does not look for the icon anymore. There is less to do on my phone. And I read books. And learn to play piano. I call my mom. And I am finding out that life is far too rich to even miss the feed.

In an old anecdote, Alexander the Great walked up to a sun bathing Diogenes, the Greek cynic philosopher that lived in a barrel, and asked him what he desired. Diogenes simply answered: "Stand out of my sun."

As surely as a guy in a barrel could tell the king to piss off, we can refuse to have our attention milked to fund somebody's private island compound. Ditch Meta’s cash cow. Get some sun.

