## Redirect

Leaving Instagram behind created space to think about what it was I was really looking for.

When an overwhelming urge to share comes over me now, I send out a couple of private messages. Have a little conversation. Check on how people are doing. I also discovered that some people actually look at the stories sections on WhatsApp and Signal. So I sometimes share there. It's cute and so it doesn't keep me or anybody else scrolling forever.

When I crave inspiration, I might reach out to some of the beautiful magazines out there. Like [MacGuffin](https://www.macguffinmagazine.com/) or [Fukt](https://www.fuktmagazine.com/). I go through them regularly and explore, engaging with the content more deeply, reading essays and interviews with the artists, look repeatedly at the same things and discover something new every time. It's so different from just swiping away to the next and next and next.

I love making photos just for the pretty pictures sake, so I became a [Wikimedia](https://commons.wikimedia.org) contributor. Now [my photos](https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?title=Special:ListFiles/Heyimada&ilshowall=1) are out there and free of copyright, so anybody can use them if they like. The spirit of the Wikipedia project, passion for information and free sharing of knowledge, resonates way more with my own values, and so contributing actually feels good.

There are more projects out there, like the [Fediverse](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fediverse), where [Mastodon](https://joinmastodon.org/) is the Twitter alternative and [pixelfed](https://pixelfed.org/) is the Instagram alternative. These projects copy the format of popular social media, but put them into the hands of the people. They still miss critical mass. But critical mass is made of people. It is up to us to make the move.

We don’t have to reach for spaces that just suck us in. We can choose where to put our attention. We can make spaces of our own.

So get out there, my dear reader. Get some sun. Choose where you put your attention. Find the spaces that are good for you, and fill them with beautiful things.

